Found it. `mapPath` is built in **unit space** (0..1 coordinates normalized by bounds), but `drawMinimap` translates/scales into **world space** (translate(-b.minX, -b.minY) after scale(sc,sc)). So the mapPath at unit coords 0..1 gets drawn at world coords 0..1 which after translate(-minX,-minY) maps to screen px at (px - minX*sc ...) — way off-screen (negative, since minX=305 and sc≈0.08 → -24px... actually it'd be near the top-left corner, tiny1x1 px region). That's why nothing shows.

The original drawMinimap (before my edit) had the same world-space transform, so this bug predates my edit — the track outline was never visible. Two options:
1. Draw mapPath in unit space: transform to unit space in drawMinimap (translate(x,y), scale(w,h), translate(-b.minX/w... ) etc.
2. Build a world-space path for the minimap.

Simplest: in drawMinimap, use a separate transform for the mapPath: after computing px,py,sc, just translate(px,py) and scale(w,h) then draw mapPath directly (unit space 0..1 fills the box exactly, ignoring the aspect-fit). But aspect fit matters — bounds aspect w/h vs box w/h differ. Since sc = min(...) fits, better to build the map path in world coordinates.

Cleanest: change buildPaths to build mapPath in world space (just the centreline), and keep the transform in drawMinimap. Let me do that — remove the normalization:

